--TEST--
`Core\Db\Rows::columns` answers what the statement described, one `Core\Db\Column` per column, so a caller has its headings before it knows whether there are rows under them — `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Asserted as *types* rather than as values, as every case over `Core\Db` is:
// nothing here reaches a server, and a member whose declared return type
// disagreed with § 18's Results table would be a diagnostic at one of the four
// assignments below.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
var $rows = $db->query("select id, owner from accounts", []);

// § 18's `->columns(): array<Column>`. Not `array<T>`: a description belongs
// to the statement, so `queryAs<Person>` describes the same columns `query`
// does and the class variable has nothing to say about it.
array<Core\Db\Column> $described = $rows->columns();

// The three readers spec § 18's Results table gives that class, written
// together because the row of the table is the claim — `getColumnMeta` is one
// array whose keys differ per driver, and this is three members that do not.
foreach ($described as Core\Db\Column $column) {
    string $name = $column->name();
    Core\Db\ColumnType $type = $column->type();
    bool $nullable = $column->nullable();
}

// And the bound that makes `columns()` a member of its own rather than a
// second reading of the rows: a description is not a row, so the two arrays
// are not assignable to one another at all.
array<Core\Db\Row> $wrong = $rows->columns();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `array<Core\Db\Row>`, found `array<Core\Db\Column>`
   --> case.nvs:26:29
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
